z3 4.3.1 bug, confirmed directly against the real binary: SMT-LIB defines no
backslash escapes at all inside a string literal (a backslash is just a
literal character), so (set-info :x "as\d") is valid input -- but z3-4.3.1's
lexer treats "\d" as an (invalid) escape anyway, reports "invalid escape
sequence", and its own quote-tracking state never recovers: the real closing
quote right after is silently absorbed rather than ending the string, and
every subsequent well-formed command is likewise consumed as more
(unterminated) string content until true EOF, confirmed by piping a small
script directly to the binary (four inputs after the "as\d" set-info all end
up swallowed into that one unterminated string, only surfacing as a final
"unexpected end of string" once stdin actually closes). In the real
interactive protocol this means the command immediately after the bad string
gets no response at all -- jSMTLIB's listen() blocks forever.

Deliberately left unfixed rather than worked around, since both directions
considered have real costs, not just implementation effort: client-side
pre-validation would mean jSMTLIB refusing genuinely valid SMT-LIB syntax
just to accommodate one buggy legacy solver version, and a general
deadlock/silence timeout in SolverProcess.listen() has no safe one-size value
-- too short risks false-positives killing a legitimately slow but correct
check-sat, too long barely improves on CI's existing coarse job-level
timeout. z3-4.3.1 (c. 2013) is a low-priority legacy compatibility target;
revisit only if this class of hang starts affecting a version that matters
more.

See https://github.com/smtlib/jSMTLIB/issues/100.
